    "description": "Cyclone Ditwah: Surgeries resume at Chilaw District General Hospital following UNFPA supported emergency repairs",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Cyclone-Ditwah-Surgeries-resume-at-Chilaw-District-General-Hospital-following-UNFPA-supported-emergency-repairs/56-787376",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1769214355.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-01-24-dailyftlk-9a897b02-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c9dc28e35013115b95157899959a6642",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Cyclone Ditwah: Surgeries resume at Chilaw District General Hospital following UNFPA supported emergency repairs\n\nSurgical services have now resumed at the District General Hospital, Chilaw, following emergency repairs supported by UNFPA in the aftermath of Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nThe rapid restoration of critical sections of the operating theatre has enabled the hospital to safely recommence sterilisation procedures and resume surgeries, ensuring continued access to life-saving maternal and newborn care for hundreds of patients each month.\n\nIn the immediate aftermath of Cyclone Ditwah, severe flooding caused extensive damage to health facilities across Sri Lanka, disrupting essential and life-saving services. Among the facilities affected was the Operating Theatre of the District General Hospital, Chilaw, a critical referral hospital serving thousands of patients each month, including a high volume of pregnant women and newborns requiring urgent care.\n\nDuring an inspection visit to the hospital in December 2025 to assess the extent of damage post-disaster, hospital staff, led by Director Dr. Sumith Attanayake, highlighted the urgent need to restore the operating theatre premises. Floodwaters had damaged the wall cladding panels, doors, and internal structures, severely compromising the sterile environment required for surgical procedures. Without immediate repairs, the hospital faced delays in resuming surgeries including essential obstetric and neonatal care.\n\n\u201cIn November, our hospital was severely affected by flooding. Our main operating theatre was among the hardest hit, and we were unable to carry out any surgical procedures. At that time, a team from UNFPA visited the hospital and saw firsthand the condition of the theatre. The side walls and internal partitions were badly damaged, putting our ability to safely resume surgeries at risk,\u201d said Dr. Attanayake."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
