    "chunk_text": "Accidental meeting with former UNchief at a UNESCO cafeteria in Paris\n\nOne rainy day in September 2003, I was having lunch at the UNESCO cafeteria. Kofi Annan was the Secretary General of the UN at that time. The cafeteria was on the top floor of the UNESCO building. The view of Paris from there was panoramic, where one could see both the Eiffel Tower and Arc de Triumph.\n\nAn elderly gentleman excused himself and sat in front of me with a soup and a baguette. Being the only two at the table, we began a conversation. My fellow diner did not have a name tag like the rest of us who were employees. He said he lived in Paris and occasionally drops in at the UNESCO for a meal. I surmised that he must be a retired former employee.\n\nAs we sat, many who passed by the table paused to chat with him and he conversed fluently in whatever language he was spoken to, be it French, Spanish, Italian, or English. A woman who spoke Spanish, bent, and kissed him.\n\nWhen I told my dining companion that I was originally from Sri Lanka, he went into raptures about Neville Kanakeratne, Shirley Amerasinghe and Lalith Athulathmudali, and lamented that none of them were around anymore. His pronunciation of Sri Lankan names was perfect.\n\nBy and by, he asked me whether I knew that Pablo Neruda lived in Ceylon for some time?. Which, of course, fortunately I knew. Neruda won the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1972 writing in Spanish and was Consul for Chile in Ceylon (now Sri Lanka) in the late 1920s.\n\nThis was an opening for him to talk about Shirley Amerasinghe\u2019s contribution to the Law of the Sea Convention. And he concluded by saying that Lalith was the brightest he had met of the Ceylonese. And then he sprung the question."
