    "chunk_text": "After We Leave Home\n\nEver since I moved to Canada, my friends, especially the ones closest to me have often asked what it\u2019s like here and what my impressions were. For some reason, they almost always assume I left because I grew tired of Sri Lanka, which couldn\u2019t be further from the truth. For a long time, I never had a sensible response for them. Perhaps I wasn\u2019t mature enough then to fully understand the predicament I was in back then. But now, after a few years have passed, I suspect it was a culmination of curiosity that brought me here. A desire to see what the outside world had to offer, the kind we grew up watching on television, listening to in music, or seeing unfold in movies along with a longing for broader cultural exposure.\n\nIt may sound naive, but it was innocent, too. But more than anything, I craved freedom. Like many people my age, I had spent years living under what I sarcastically called the \u201cwrath of a school regime.\u201d However, I also realized that very period, once felt suffocating has now slowly become a memory I return to fondly, every now and then.\n\nHaving my childhood spent entirely in Sri Lanka and some time here in North America, I think it\u2019s safe to say that schools here feel noticeably plainer than ours. If I had to compare them to a delicacy, I\u2019d say North American schools resembled something like a regular pancake, consistent, mild in flavor, and guaranteed to get the job done. Schools in Sri Lanka, on the other hand, were like those traditional pancakes, crisp on the outside, soft in texture, and filled with a sweetness laced with a hint of spice. They also come in many shapes and forms, sometimes inconsistent, but exceptional when the recipe was right."
  },
