    "chunk_text": "Having my childhood spent entirely in Sri Lanka and some time here in North America, I think it\u2019s safe to say that schools here feel noticeably plainer than ours. If I had to compare them to a delicacy, I\u2019d say North American schools resembled something like a regular pancake, consistent, mild in flavor, and guaranteed to get the job done. Schools in Sri Lanka, on the other hand, were like those traditional pancakes, crisp on the outside, soft in texture, and filled with a sweetness laced with a hint of spice. They also come in many shapes and forms, sometimes inconsistent, but exceptional when the recipe was right.\n\n\u201cYou talk too much about school. You\u2019re not a kid anymore,\u201d my parents would joke, often placing extra emphasis on the word kid, more than I would\u2019ve liked. Unfortunately for me, these comments usually surfaced when they were hearing my treasured stories for the millionth time. \u201cFine,\u201d I\u2019d yell back. \u201cI\u2019m never telling those stories again,\u201d I\u2019d declare, locking myself in my room as if to make a point, knowing fully well that both my parents and I were aware that I\u2019d bring them up again in less than a week.\n\nI remember a one moment when a friend of mine in Canada was astonished after seeing a photo of me in my school uniform. She had begged me to share it after I saw hers. Having never been required to wear a uniform herself, she was utterly bedazzled.\n\n\u201cThat\u2019s you?\u201d she shouted, what sounded like all caps inside our college union hall.\n\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that hair?\u201d she asked, trying her best to mask her laughter.\n\n\u201cThat\u2019s how they wanted us to do it, okay?\u201d I replied defensively, justifying what, in her opinion, was one of the most unfortunate hairstyles of the twenty-first century.\n\n\u201cOur school,\u201d I said, knowing fully well that someone who could voluntarily wear pajamas to school had no real concept of how extreme, sometimes absurd our school system could be."
  },
