    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "As time passed, I noticed that many of my school friends, like me, are scattered across different countries soon after graduating, a trend that\u2019s only grown stronger in recent years. When we talk now, the one common thread is how much we miss the simple act of planning to hang out, something that has since become occasion worthy. We often laugh about childhood stories, like the afternoon one of our daredevil friends fell into an abandoned pond at school while searching for the \u201cgoldfish\u201d she claimed to have seen the day before.\n\n\u201cHelp me!\u201d she screamed, as a loud splash confirmed our worst fear.\n\n\u201cTadpoles. That\u2019s all they\u2019ve got here, nothing else,\u201d she muttered afterward, drenched, shaken, and trying to hide her embarrassment. Luckily, we found a spare uniform in the first-aid room, and the incident went unnoticed by teachers. Years later ironically, that same friend went on to became one of the best swimmers in school and is now preparing to get married later this year, reminding how fleeting time truly is.\n\nAs I grow older, there\u2019s one quote that rings truer with each passing day, \u201ctime waits for no one.\u201d For many, moving abroad is a dream, a strategic decision, or both. What often goes unspoken, however, is the quiet cost, the family events, milestones, and everyday moments missed back home. Even when people pretend to be unfazed, the absence can weigh heavily, even on the strongest among us.\n\nAnd once we leave our native land, everything connected to it begins to feel slightly enchanted. Even in photographs, the sun looks brighter, the sky bluer, and smiles wider. Perhaps it\u2019s nostalgia that tightens our grip on memory, making us long, even for a short visit, hoping that the same charm still lingers in the places we now carry with us.\n\nThe writer is a Sri lankan, currently resides in Toronto, Canada. She may be contacted at \u2018dahamisamarathunga44@gmail.com)\n\nPat