    "chunk_size_bytes": 1871,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan Scholar Teaching Hindi at University of Michigan\n\nIn a historic appointment for Sri Lankan academia, a Sri Lankan educator is teaching Hindi at a foreign university for the first time. Last October Ridma Nishadinee Lansakkara, of the National Institute of Language Education and Training (NILET), received an appointment to the faculty of the University of Michigan, a first-class overseas institution as a Hindi instructor.\n\nRidma\u2019s journey from a curious child in Kurunegala to a faculty member at a prestigious American university is a testament to dedication and innovative scholarship. It is doubly remarkable, as Hindi academia is a highly competitive arena, even for native speakers.\n\nRidma\u2019s passion for Hindi began not in a classroom, but through the vibrant world of Indian cinema and music. Growing up in a multilingual Sinhala and Tamil-speaking environment, she was drawn to the language\u2019s expressiveness. \u201cThat early curiosity became the foundation of my academic journey,\u201d she recalls.\n\nHer formal training began at Maliyadewa Girls\u2019 College in Kurunegala, continuing at the Hindi Sansthan Sri Lanka. Her exceptional abilities earned her a scholarship from the Indian government in 2009 to study at the Central Institute of Hindi in Agra. She later graduated top of her class with a Special Degree in Hindi from the University of Kelaniya in 2013. Subsequently, in 2017 she obtained an MPhil in Hindi from Kelaniya, at which institution she is, currently, completing her PhD thesis.\n\nPrior to her international appointment, Ridma built a robust career at home. She served as a Temporary and Visiting Lecturer at the University of Kelaniya from 2013 to 2018. In 2018, she joined NILET as an Academic and Research Officer, simultaneously serving as a Visiting Lecturer in Hindi at the Universities of Colombo, Ruhuna, and Sabaragamuwa."
  },
