    "md5": "3334edb3e8d427e405c2ce78e7d68d0a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "Prior to her international appointment, Ridma built a robust career at home. She served as a Temporary and Visiting Lecturer at the University of Kelaniya from 2013 to 2018. In 2018, she joined NILET as an Academic and Research Officer, simultaneously serving as a Visiting Lecturer in Hindi at the Universities of Colombo, Ruhuna, and Sabaragamuwa.\n\nFaced with limited opportunities within the local university system, Ridma began applying internationally. Her persistent scholarship, publications, and a student-centred teaching philosophy which emphasises cultural context, role-play, and real-world application, ultimately secured her the position at the University of Michigan.\n\nThe transition presented challenges, from adapting to a new academic culture to being far from family. \u201cProfessionally, I had to adapt my teaching to students with diverse backgrounds and learning styles,\u201d she states. \u201cOver time, I learned to navigate these differences and transform them into strengths.\u201d\n\nRidma views her role as both a privilege and a responsibility. \u201cI want to show that Sri Lanka contributes meaningfully to global scholarship and that our multilingual heritage enriches conversations about South Asia,\u201d she says. She considers the appointment a milestone for Hindi studies in Sri Lanka, particularly as a non-native speaker.\n\nShe remains a passionate advocate for foreign language education for Sri Lankan students, stressing that languages create pathways to international education, careers, and cross-cultural empathy. Her advice to students is to \u201cstart early, be consistent, and use media you genuinely enjoy.\u201d\n\nRidma extends her gratitude to her family, her sole mentor in Sri Lanka, Senior Professor Upul Ranjith Hewawitanagamage, and her husband for their unwavering support. She also thanks the Indian Council for Cultural Relations (ICCR) for funding her pivotal studies in India."
