    "chunk_text": "Social media presents a different but related experience. Platforms like Instagram, TikTok, and Twitter are filled with content that updates constantly. Users scroll through photos, short videos, text posts, and advertisements. The design of these platforms also uses features that encourage prolonged use. The infinite scroll function allows users to swipe up or down to see more content without reaching an end. Unlike television episodes that have a beginning and an end, social media feeds have no natural conclusion. The stream of content continues as long as the user wishes to scroll. This structure taps into a psychological response that rewards novelty. Every new post offers something slightly different, and the anticipation of discovering the next interesting or entertaining item keeps people scrolling.\n\nThere is also a reward mechanism at work in the brain. Engaging with content that is funny, dramatic, or emotionally resonant triggers the release of dopamine, a chemical associated with pleasure and reward. When people binge watch or scroll through social media, they experience bursts of positive feelings. These sensations encourage them to repeat the behavior in search of more rewards. Over time, the brain learns to associate screen time with pleasure, making it more difficult to disengage. This is similar to how people develop habits. The more often the behavior is repeated, the stronger the association becomes. For many, reaching for their phone or turning on a show becomes an automatic response in moments of boredom or stress."
