    "chunk_text": "There is also a reward mechanism at work in the brain. Engaging with content that is funny, dramatic, or emotionally resonant triggers the release of dopamine, a chemical associated with pleasure and reward. When people binge watch or scroll through social media, they experience bursts of positive feelings. These sensations encourage them to repeat the behavior in search of more rewards. Over time, the brain learns to associate screen time with pleasure, making it more difficult to disengage. This is similar to how people develop habits. The more often the behavior is repeated, the stronger the association becomes. For many, reaching for their phone or turning on a show becomes an automatic response in moments of boredom or stress.\n\nThere is also a cultural component. Binge watching entire seasons of a popular series has become a shared social experience. People talk about the latest shows with friends, coworkers, and on social media. This creates a cultural pressure to watch certain shows quickly. People do not want to be left out of conversations about plot twists or character developments. In the same way, social media habits can be fueled by the desire to stay up to date. Friends may post photos from events, share opinions on current news, or post humorous content. Users are motivated to keep up with these posts so they feel connected and informed. Missing out can make people feel excluded or out of sync with their social circles."
