    "md5": "d0875e3905e98501e7085cb99d2315b1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1706,
    "chunk_text": "You cannot avoid bitter arguments in a marriage all the time. In fact, arguments are necessary to release your tension. What is more critical is how you argue. Most couples argue with each other because their daily life is not stimulating enough. Some people believe that spouses argue because they are not sexually satisfied. This is another myth. Sex comprises about 10 per cent of what is important in a marriage. However, if there is no sexual satisfaction, it may turn out to be a big issue. According to psychologists, there should be a sexual bond to make you feel that you are more than friends. You are mates \u2013 soul mates!\n\nWhile staying in a house as a paying guest, a young man noticed the landlady\u2019s disturbing traits and her persistent criticism of her husband. She apparently loved him but in relationships peevishness often directs itself to what is closest at hand. Her digs included, \u201cRex, you\u2019re so antisocial. You\u2019re a fixture in front of the idiot box. Sometimes he pretended to ignore her tirades or would growl, \u201cOh, Mary for God\u2019s sake, cut it out.\u201d One day Mary told her paying guest that her husband never listened to her. Their shouting and arguments continued confirming the old saying, \u201cConditioned patterns die hard.\u201d\n\nIn any relationship the feeling of rejection, of being unaccepted or discarded can be the most painful version of not getting what you want from the other partner. You may be rejected for a variety of reasons. Some rejections are bigger than others. They can range from a simple no to a snub or a betrayal. Psychologists advise people to think of rejection as God\u2019s protection. Try to find the meaning of a rejection in a wider context."
  },
